{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019CdBdfvMqiuYnMMdMfnFjZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QprabZCkkXQP6cEHHGFc4H","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a blind peer review for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper: \"What Do Labeling Functions Reveal About Agent Stagnation? An Empirical Study of Weak Supervision for Sequential Agent Behavior\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nThe paper applies data programming (weak supervision) to detect stagnation in LLM agent trajectories. Three labeling functions (LFs) are designed: command_repetition, file_diff_delta, outcome_backpropagation. A GRU-based probe is trained on majority-vote labels from these LFs over 54,809 windows from SWE-bench agent traces.\n\nThe paper's core narrative is **negative results**: all three original claims fail:\n1. High-confidence subset performance (conf≥2, macro-F1 0.91) does NOT generalize to full data (0.74) or human gold labels (0.68)\n2. The probe does NOT outperform simple heuristics — on gold labels, Repeat-3 (F1 0.72) beats probe (0.68)\n3. Cross-agent transfer from SWE-agent to OpenHands degrades by 12pp (0.74→0.62), barely beating Repeat-3 by 1pp\n\nPositive finding: LF temporal dynamics analysis reveals a \"stagnation onset signature\" (command_repetition leads by 1.8 steps, file_diff_delta concurrent, outcome_backpropagation lags by 2.3 steps) and 4 distinct transition patterns covering 87% of episodes.\n\n### Key Results Tables\n\n**Table 1: conf≥2 vs full data**\n- conf≥2: Train 15,401, Macro-F1 0.9055, Stag-F1 0.8593, Prog-F1 0.9518, vs Repeat-3 +6.24pp\n- conf≥1 (full): Train 54,809, Macro-F1 0.74, Stag-F1 0.67, Prog-F1 0.81, vs Repeat-3 +1.2pp\n- Δ: -16.55pp macro, -18.93pp stag, -14.18pp prog, -5.04pp vs Repeat-3\n\n**Table 2: Probe vs LF Oracle vs baselines**\n- LF Oracle: conf≥2 F1 0.92, Full F1 0.73, No training cost, Full interpretability\n- GRU Probe: conf≥2 F1 0.9055, Full F1 0.74, 0.5 GPU-hr, Low interpretability\n- Repeat-3: conf≥2 F1 0.8432, Full F1 0.728\n\n**Table 3: Human gold label evaluation (400 windows, 3 annotators)**\n- GRU Probe (conf≥2): Gold-F1 0.78, LF-label F1 0.9055, Δ -12.55pp\n- GRU Probe (full): Gold-F1 0.68, LF-label F1 0.74, Δ -6pp\n- Repeat-3: Gold-F1 0.72, LF-label F1 0.8432, Δ -12.32pp\n- LF Oracle: Gold-F1 0.65, LF-label F1 0.92, Δ -27pp\n- LLM Judge (few-shot): Gold-F1 0.84\n\n**Table 4: Inter-annotator agreement**\n- Overall κ=0.52, Clear progress κ=0.68, Clear stagnation κ=0.61, Semantic stagnation κ=0.38, Ambiguous κ=0.29\n\n**Table 5: LLM-as-Judge vs probe**\n- GPT-4o few-shot: Macro-F1 0.86, Stag-F1 0.80, Latency 4100ms, Cost/1k $18.20\n- GRU Probe (full): Macro-F1 0.74, Stag-F1 0.67, Latency 4.5ms, Cost/1k $0.08\n\n**Table 6: Cost-accuracy Pareto** (annual cost at 50k windows)\n- GRU Probe: F1 0.74, $4/yr; LF Oracle: F1 0.73, $0; Repeat-3: F1 0.728, $0; LLM Judge: F1 0.86, $910/yr\n\n**Table 7: GRU vs Mean-pool+LR**\n- GRU: conf≥2 0.9055, Full 0.74, ~530K params, 28 min\n- Mean-pool+LR: conf≥2 0.87, Full 0.72, ~1K params, 12 sec (gap: +2pp on full, not significant p≈0.08-0.15)\n\n**Table 8: NLP vs Non-NLP features**\n- GRU (NLP, 1024-d embeddings): conf≥2 0.9055, Full 0.74\n- GBM (Non-NLP, 12 structural features): conf≥2 0.83, Full 0.70 (gap: +4pp)\n\n**Table 9: Cross-agent transfer**\n- SWE-agent full in-domain: 0.74; OpenHands zero-shot: 0.62 (Stag-F1 0.53); OpenHands Repeat-3: 0.61\n\n**Table 11: Error distribution by confidence**\n- conf=1: 39,408 windows, 12,216 errors, rate 0.31, 71% of total errors\n- conf=2: 14,609 windows, 1,315 errors, rate 0.09, 26%\n- conf=3: 792 windows, 24 errors, rate 0.03, 3%\n\n**Table 12: FN decomposition**: Semantic stagnation 58%, Late-onset 24%, Ambiguous boundary 18%\n\n**Table 13: LF temporal ordering**: cmd_rep leads -1.8 steps (activation 0.78), file_diff concurrent -0.5 (0.71), outcome_backprop lags +2.3 (0.64)\n\n**Table 14: Stagnation transition patterns**: Type A cmd→file→outcome 42%, Type B file→cmd→outcome 18%, Type C cmd only 15%, Type D outcome→file→cmd 12%\n\n**Tables 15-21**: Ablations (LF hold-one-out, window size, architecture, SupCon, data scaling, representation quality, early termination)\n\n### Review Context: Imagined Experiments Coverage\nThe review context lists 14 planned experiments. The paper covers ALL of them:\n1. Full data training (conf≥1) — Section 4, Table 1 ✓\n2. LF Ensemble Oracle — Section 4.1, Table 2 ✓\n3. LLM-as-Judge — Section 5.1, Tables 5-6 ✓\n4. Mean-Pool+LR — Section 5.2, Table 7 ✓\n5. Non-NLP Baseline — Section 5.3, Table 8 ✓\n6. Cross-Agent Transfer — Section 6, Tables 9-10 ✓\n7. Error Analysis — Section 7, Tables 11-12 ✓\n8. LF Temporal Dynamics — Section 8, Tables 13-14 ✓\n9. Window Size Ablation — Section 9.1, Table 16 ✓\n10. Architecture Ablation — Section 9.2, Table 17 ✓\n11. LF Hold-One-Out — Section 8.3, Table 15 ✓\n12. SupCon Ablation — Section 9.3, Table 18 ✓\n13. Data Scaling — Section 9.4, Table 19 ✓\n14. Early Termination Simulation — Section 10, Table 21 ✓\n15. Interpretability/Representation Quality — Section 9.5, Table 20 ✓\n16. Human Annotation Gold Set — Section 4.2, Tables 3-4 ✓\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper focusing on:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n- Is the weak supervision framework well-designed? Are the 3 LFs sufficient/well-motivated?\n- Is the confidence-based filtering (conf≥2 vs full) properly analyzed?\n- Is the GRU probe architecture appropriate? Is the \"deliberately simple\" justification convincing?\n- Is the human annotation protocol (400 windows, 3 annotators, Fleiss κ) adequate?\n- Are statistical claims properly supported (e.g., bootstrap resampling for significance)?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are baselines comprehensive and fair? (Repeat-3, LF Oracle, LLM-as-Judge, Mean-pool+LR, Non-NLP GBM)\n- Are ablations thorough? (window size, architecture, SupCon, LF hold-out, data scaling)\n- Is the cross-agent transfer evaluation adequate? (SWE-agent → OpenHands)\n- Is the error analysis insightful? (conf-stratified errors, FN taxonomy)\n- Is the early termination simulation convincing?\n- Are there missing experiments or controls?\n\n### 3. Clarity of Negative Results\n- Is the paper honest and rigorous about what failed and why?\n- Is the \"selection bias\" diagnosis well-supported?\n- Does the discussion of \"what went wrong\" (Section 11) provide genuine insight?\n\n### 4. Strength of Positive Findings\n- Is the LF temporal dynamics analysis (Section 8) methodologically sound?\n- Are the 4 stagnation transition patterns well-supported (87% coverage)?\n- Is this finding genuinely independent of probe performance?\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Weaknesses\n- Compare against the review context's 14 imagined experiments — are all covered?\n- Are there important controls or experiments the paper should have included but didn't?\n- Are there any methodological red flags?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Minor Issues\n- Overall Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14154,"cache_read_input_tokens":38937,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14154},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"16ab1c0f-3ad5-4e32-8f39-ad0da20f8dab","uuid":"c193613a-432c-49c9-9186-ec020cfc634b"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ac3bcdd59bd9b54f1","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QprabZCkkXQP6cEHHGFc4H","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a blind peer review for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper: \"What Do Labeling Functions Reveal About Agent Stagnation? An Empirical Study of Weak Supervision for Sequential Agent Behavior\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nThe paper applies data programming (weak supervision) to detect stagnation in LLM agent trajectories. Three labeling functions (LFs) are designed: command_repetition, file_diff_delta, outcome_backpropagation. A GRU-based probe is trained on majority-vote labels from these LFs over 54,809 windows from SWE-bench agent traces.\n\nThe paper's core narrative is **negative results**: all three original claims fail:\n1. High-confidence subset performance (conf≥2, macro-F1 0.91) does NOT generalize to full data (0.74) or human gold labels (0.68)\n2. The probe does NOT outperform simple heuristics — on gold labels, Repeat-3 (F1 0.72) beats probe (0.68)\n3. Cross-agent transfer from SWE-agent to OpenHands degrades by 12pp (0.74→0.62), barely beating Repeat-3 by 1pp\n\nPositive finding: LF temporal dynamics analysis reveals a \"stagnation onset signature\" (command_repetition leads by 1.8 steps, file_diff_delta concurrent, outcome_backpropagation lags by 2.3 steps) and 4 distinct transition patterns covering 87% of episodes.\n\n### Key Results Tables\n\n**Table 1: conf≥2 vs full data**\n- conf≥2: Train 15,401, Macro-F1 0.9055, Stag-F1 0.8593, Prog-F1 0.9518, vs Repeat-3 +6.24pp\n- conf≥1 (full): Train 54,809, Macro-F1 0.74, Stag-F1 0.67, Prog-F1 0.81, vs Repeat-3 +1.2pp\n- Δ: -16.55pp macro, -18.93pp stag, -14.18pp prog, -5.04pp vs Repeat-3\n\n**Table 2: Probe vs LF Oracle vs baselines**\n- LF Oracle: conf≥2 F1 0.92, Full F1 0.73, No training cost, Full interpretability\n- GRU Probe: conf≥2 F1 0.9055, Full F1 0.74, 0.5 GPU-hr, Low interpretability\n- Repeat-3: conf≥2 F1 0.8432, Full F1 0.728\n\n**Table 3: Human gold label evaluation (400 windows, 3 annotators)**\n- GRU Probe (conf≥2): Gold-F1 0.78, LF-label F1 0.9055, Δ -12.55pp\n- GRU Probe (full): Gold-F1 0.68, LF-label F1 0.74, Δ -6pp\n- Repeat-3: Gold-F1 0.72, LF-label F1 0.8432, Δ -12.32pp\n- LF Oracle: Gold-F1 0.65, LF-label F1 0.92, Δ -27pp\n- LLM Judge (few-shot): Gold-F1 0.84\n\n**Table 4: Inter-annotator agreement**\n- Overall κ=0.52, Clear progress κ=0.68, Clear stagnation κ=0.61, Semantic stagnation κ=0.38, Ambiguous κ=0.29\n\n**Table 5: LLM-as-Judge vs probe**\n- GPT-4o few-shot: Macro-F1 0.86, Stag-F1 0.80, Latency 4100ms, Cost/1k $18.20\n- GRU Probe (full): Macro-F1 0.74, Stag-F1 0.67, Latency 4.5ms, Cost/1k $0.08\n\n**Table 6: Cost-accuracy Pareto** (annual cost at 50k windows)\n- GRU Probe: F1 0.74, $4/yr; LF Oracle: F1 0.73, $0; Repeat-3: F1 0.728, $0; LLM Judge: F1 0.86, $910/yr\n\n**Table 7: GRU vs Mean-pool+LR**\n- GRU: conf≥2 0.9055, Full 0.74, ~530K params, 28 min\n- Mean-pool+LR: conf≥2 0.87, Full 0.72, ~1K params, 12 sec (gap: +2pp on full, not significant p≈0.08-0.15)\n\n**Table 8: NLP vs Non-NLP features**\n- GRU (NLP, 1024-d embeddings): conf≥2 0.9055, Full 0.74\n- GBM (Non-NLP, 12 structural features): conf≥2 0.83, Full 0.70 (gap: +4pp)\n\n**Table 9: Cross-agent transfer**\n- SWE-agent full in-domain: 0.74; OpenHands zero-shot: 0.62 (Stag-F1 0.53); OpenHands Repeat-3: 0.61\n\n**Table 11: Error distribution by confidence**\n- conf=1: 39,408 windows, 12,216 errors, rate 0.31, 71% of total errors\n- conf=2: 14,609 windows, 1,315 errors, rate 0.09, 26%\n- conf=3: 792 windows, 24 errors, rate 0.03, 3%\n\n**Table 12: FN decomposition**: Semantic stagnation 58%, Late-onset 24%, Ambiguous boundary 18%\n\n**Table 13: LF temporal ordering**: cmd_rep leads -1.8 steps (activation 0.78), file_diff concurrent -0.5 (0.71), outcome_backprop lags +2.3 (0.64)\n\n**Table 14: Stagnation transition patterns**: Type A cmd→file→outcome 42%, Type B file→cmd→outcome 18%, Type C cmd only 15%, Type D outcome→file→cmd 12%\n\n**Tables 15-21**: Ablations (LF hold-one-out, window size, architecture, SupCon, data scaling, representation quality, early termination)\n\n### Review Context: Imagined Experiments Coverage\nThe review context lists 14 planned experiments. The paper covers ALL of them:\n1. Full data training (conf≥1) — Section 4, Table 1 ✓\n2. LF Ensemble Oracle — Section 4.1, Table 2 ✓\n3. LLM-as-Judge — Section 5.1, Tables 5-6 ✓\n4. Mean-Pool+LR — Section 5.2, Table 7 ✓\n5. Non-NLP Baseline — Section 5.3, Table 8 ✓\n6. Cross-Agent Transfer — Section 6, Tables 9-10 ✓\n7. Error Analysis — Section 7, Tables 11-12 ✓\n8. LF Temporal Dynamics — Section 8, Tables 13-14 ✓\n9. Window Size Ablation — Section 9.1, Table 16 ✓\n10. Architecture Ablation — Section 9.2, Table 17 ✓\n11. LF Hold-One-Out — Section 8.3, Table 15 ✓\n12. SupCon Ablation — Section 9.3, Table 18 ✓\n13. Data Scaling — Section 9.4, Table 19 ✓\n14. Early Termination Simulation — Section 10, Table 21 ✓\n15. Interpretability/Representation Quality — Section 9.5, Table 20 ✓\n16. Human Annotation Gold Set — Section 4.2, Tables 3-4 ✓\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper focusing on:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n- Is the weak supervision framework well-designed? Are the 3 LFs sufficient/well-motivated?\n- Is the confidence-based filtering (conf≥2 vs full) properly analyzed?\n- Is the GRU probe architecture appropriate? Is the \"deliberately simple\" justification convincing?\n- Is the human annotation protocol (400 windows, 3 annotators, Fleiss κ) adequate?\n- Are statistical claims properly supported (e.g., bootstrap resampling for significance)?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are baselines comprehensive and fair? (Repeat-3, LF Oracle, LLM-as-Judge, Mean-pool+LR, Non-NLP GBM)\n- Are ablations thorough? (window size, architecture, SupCon, LF hold-out, data scaling)\n- Is the cross-agent transfer evaluation adequate? (SWE-agent → OpenHands)\n- Is the error analysis insightful? (conf-stratified errors, FN taxonomy)\n- Is the early termination simulation convincing?\n- Are there missing experiments or controls?\n\n### 3. Clarity of Negative Results\n- Is the paper honest and rigorous about what failed and why?\n- Is the \"selection bias\" diagnosis well-supported?\n- Does the discussion of \"what went wrong\" (Section 11) provide genuine insight?\n\n### 4. Strength of Positive Findings\n- Is the LF temporal dynamics analysis (Section 8) methodologically sound?\n- Are the 4 stagnation transition patterns well-supported (87% coverage)?\n- Is this finding genuinely independent of probe performance?\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Weaknesses\n- Compare against the review context's 14 imagined experiments — are all covered?\n- Are there important controls or experiments the paper should have included but didn't?\n- Are there any methodological red flags?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Minor Issues\n- Overall Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)","uuid":"5478d9ae-26d1-4caa-a45d-4a54ff78ee63","session_id":"16ab1c0f-3ad5-4e32-8f39-ad0da20f8dab"}
