{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014mDRYaHfiz6zZuEcqNu9eA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T081656\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T08:57:23.283843+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: ProgressProbe\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n11\t**Dream ID:** 20260514T081656\n12\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n13\t**Date:** 2026-05-14\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## 1. Paper Summary\n18\t\n19\tThe paper proposes ProgressProbe, a framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories — situations where agents execute diverse but collectively ineffective actions. The framework has two components: (1) a data-programming pipeline that transfers Snorkel's paradigm to sequential trajectory data using three domain-specific labeling functions (file_diff_delta, command_repetition, outcome_backpropagation), producing window-level progress/stagnation labels via majority vote; and (2) a lightweight GRU probe with attention (1.32M params) trained on these noisy labels. On SWE-bench agent trajectories, the probe achieves 0.906 macro-F1 on majority-vote labels and 0.862 on a 400-window human gold set, outperforming baselines including LF oracle (+4.43pp), Repeat-3 heuristic (+6.89pp on gold), and Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot (+3.74pp at 31× lower cost). Cross-agent transfer from SWE-agent to OpenHands retains 0.835 F1. Simulated early termination saves 32.7% of tokens while preserving 96.1% task success.\n20\t\n21\tThe paper covers 16 experiments including main results, 7 baselines, 6 ablations, error analysis, cross-agent transfer, LF temporal dynamics, and a downstream early-termination application.\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## 2. Reviewer Reports\n26\t\n27\t### Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods + Experiments\n28\t\n29\t**Strengths:**\n30\t1. **Comprehensive experimental coverage** — 16 experiments leaving few obvious gaps\n31\t2. **Circularity addressed** via 400-window human gold set (Fleiss κ=0.724) as primary evaluation\n32\t3. **Strong baseline suite** including LLM-as-Judge (Claude S4, GPT-4o) with cost-accuracy Pareto analysis\n33\t4. **Instance-level data splitting** prevents cross-contamination between train/test windows from the same trajectory\n34\t5. **Honest architectural positioning** — architecture framed as engineering choice, not contribution; supported by ablation showing GRU ≈ Transformer (< 1pp difference)\n35\t6. **Informative error analysis** — FP/FN decomposition with meaningful patterns (exploratory behavior vs. diverse ineffective commands)\n36\t7. **LF temporal dynamics** — leading/lagging behavior of LFs at stagnation onset (34% → 71% agreement) is a genuinely interesting observation for the data programming community\n37\t\n38\t**Weaknesses:**\n39\t1. **(Critical) Sentence encoder unnamed.** The paper says \"a frozen sentence encoder\" with 1024 dimensions but never identifies it. Code inspection reveals it is Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B with last-token pooling. This is a basic reproducibility failure. No embedding model ablation is provided — results may differ substantially with different encoders.\n40\t2. **(Critical) No variance reporting.** All results are from a single seed (42). With margins of 2–7pp over baselines and a small gold set, it is impossible to assess statistical reliability. Multi-seed experiments are absent.\n41\t3. **(Critical) Code-paper architecture discrepancy.** The paper describes a BiGRU with learned attention weights (Equations 2–3, Section 3.4) and reports attention-based interpretability results (concentration coefficient 0.73, stagnation onset localization at steps 2–3). **Code inspection reveals the actual model is a unidirectional 2-layer GRU that takes the last hidden state with no attention mechanism whatsoever.** This means either the paper describes a model different from what was evaluated, or the attention-based interpretability claims (Section 7) are unsupported. This is the most serious finding of this review.\n42\t4. **(Major) outcome_backpropagation LF leaks future information.** This LF propagates final trajectory outcome backward to label earlier windows. For exit_context trajectories, ALL windows are labeled as stagnation regardless of actual content. This creates a risk that the probe learns trajectory-position features as a proxy for genuine progress signals.\n43\t5. **(Major) Human gold set too small.** 400 windows (156 stag / 244 prog) yields ~±3–4pp bootstrap CI on macro-F1. The +2.11pp advantage over Claude S4 few-shot is likely not statistically significant at this sample size. No confidence intervals are reported.\n44\t6. **(Moderate) Repeat-3 is a straw-man.** Repeat-3 uses exact string matching. Real cycle detectors use embedding similarity or learned anomaly detection. A soft-match Repeat-3 variant would likely close part of the gap.\n45\t7. **(Moderate) Cross-agent evaluation uses unvalidated labels.** The 48,352 OpenHands windows are labeled by the same LFs (with format adaptation). Without any human labels on OpenHands, the 0.835 F1 may reflect ability to mimic adapted LFs rather than genuine progress detection.\n46\t8. **(Minor) Class imbalance.** 71.5% progress / 28.5% stagnation. MV labels systematically under-label stagnation (38 stag→prog errors vs. 24 prog→stag in gold set). Per-class precision/recall not reported for most tables.\n47\t\n48\t**R1 Questions to Authors:**\n49\t1. What is the frozen sentence encoder? Why was it chosen? How sensitive are results to this choice?\n50\t2. Which model produced the reported numbers — the attention model described in the paper or the simpler GRU in the code?\n51\t3. Multi-seed variance results?\n52\t4. How were the 400 gold windows sampled?\n53\t5. How was the early-termination \"optimal threshold\" criterion defined?\n54\t6. What prompt template was used for LLM-as-Judge?\n55\t7. What fraction of training data comes from exit_context trajectories?\n56\t\n57\t**R1 Scores:** Soundness 3/5 | Presentation 4/5 | Contribution 3/5 | **Overall 3/6 (Weak Reject)** | Confidence 4/5\n58\t\n59\t---\n60\t\n61\t### Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty + Related Work\n62\t\n63\t**Strengths:**\n64\t1. **Problem identification is practically valuable** — semantic stagnation is a genuine pain point in deployed agent systems\n65\t2. **\"Student exceeds teacher\" effect** (+4.43pp over LF oracle) is the cleanest conceptual contribution, demonstrating GRU captures temporal dependencies LFs miss\n66\t3. **Cross-agent transfer** provides meaningful generalization evidence\n67\t4. **Cost-efficiency analysis** (31× cheaper, 606× faster, higher F1 vs. LLM judges) gives immediate practical relevance\n68\t\n69\t**Weaknesses:**\n70\t1. **(Critical) Novelty is thin.** Both components are straightforward applications of existing techniques: (a) Snorkel majority-vote aggregation with three hand-written threshold heuristics, (b) a standard GRU classifier. Neither involves methodological innovation. The \"first to apply data programming to sequential trajectory data\" is a narrow priority claim; the sequential extension is handled by the downstream GRU, not by any modification to the data programming framework.\n71\t2. **(Critical) Related work severely incomplete.** Only 10 references — far below the 30–50 standard for a top venue. Critical missing areas:\n72\t   - **Process Reward Models (PRMs):** Lightman et al. (2023) \"Let's Verify Step by Step,\" Math-Shepherd — these estimate step-level correctness/progress in reasoning chains, which is conceptually the same task\n73\t   - **RL value functions:** Any RL agent framework learns a value function estimating expected future reward — literally a progress estimator. CodeRL, RLHF-based agent training\n74\t   - **Agent scaffolding with self-reflection:** Reflexion (Shinn et al. 2023), LATS (Zhou et al. 2023) — detect agent failure and intervene\n75\t   - **Execution feedback:** Test-pass-rate signals in SWE-bench and follow-ups\n76\t   - **Trajectory anomaly detection** beyond the two papers cited\n77\t   - **Weak supervision beyond Snorkel:** MeTaL, FlyingSquid, programmatic labeling advances\n78\t3. **(Major) \"Semantic stagnation\" may rebrand existing concepts.** The RL literature studies \"plateaus\"; the agent literature discusses \"looping\" and \"unproductive exploration.\" The paper does not adequately argue why this requires a new term versus positioning within existing vocabulary (exploration-exploitation failure).\n79\t4. **(Moderate) LLM-as-Judge comparison underspecified.** One prompt template against two models does not constitute a thorough comparison with the LLM-based monitoring paradigm. Chain-of-thought, multi-turn evaluation, and prompt optimization are known to substantially improve LLM judgment.\n80\t5. **(Major) Domain specificity undermines generality.** All three LFs are hand-crafted for SWE-bench coding agents. No evaluation on non-coding agent tasks (WebArena, ToolBench, mathematical reasoning). Cross-agent transfer within the same task distribution (SWE-bench) is a weak form of generalization.\n81\t6. **(Moderate) Venue fit concern.** The problem and solution are deeply embedded in SE agent evaluation. The Section 6.5 non-NLP baseline (0.723 F1 for structural features) argues for NLP relevance, but the entire framework (LFs, evaluation, application) is SE-specific.\n82\t\n83\t**R2 Questions to Authors:**\n84\t1. How does ProgressProbe differ from training a PRM on agent trajectories?\n85\t2. Could a simple value function trained with TD learning achieve similar performance?\n86\t3. What is the concrete plan for extending to non-coding domains?\n87\t4. Why were only 10 references included?\n88\t5. What embedding model is used?\n89\t6. Was logistic regression on combined handcrafted features + LF outputs tried?\n90\t\n91\t**R2 Scores:** Soundness 3/5 | Presentation 3/5 | Contribution 2/5 | **Overall 3/6 (Reject)** | Confidence 4/5\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 3. Roundtable Discussion\n96\t\n97\t### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n98\t\n99\t**R1 after reading R2:**\n100\t- Agrees with R2's related work critique (W2) as a significant oversight — PRM literature is particularly relevant since ProgressProbe is functionally a PRM trained via weak supervision\n101\t- Agrees that domain specificity (W5–W6) reinforces generalizability concerns\n102\t- Partially disagrees with W3 (semantic stagnation as rebrand): the operationalization via embedding similarity over sliding windows is concrete and measurable, not merely relabeling\n103\t- Thinks R2 underweights the \"student exceeds teacher\" effect\n104\t- **Maintains 3/6**\n105\t\n106\t**R2 after reading R1:**\n107\t- Considers R1's W3 (code-paper discrepancy) the most damaging finding in either review\n108\t- Agrees with all of R1's critical weaknesses (unnamed encoder, single seed, future leakage, small gold set)\n109\t- Disagrees with R1's Presentation 4/5 — a paper describing an architecture different from its implementation has a presentation problem, not just soundness\n110\t- Thinks R1 overweights LF temporal dynamics as \"genuinely novel\" — it is a descriptive finding, not a methodological contribution\n111\t- **Lowers score to 2/6 (Strong Reject)** due to compounded concerns about reproducibility and accuracy\n112\t\n113\t### Step 3: Convergence\n114\t\n115\tBoth reviewers converge on the following critical issues:\n116\t1. **Code-paper discrepancy is disqualifying in current form** — the BiGRU+attention described in the paper does not exist in the codebase; all attention-based interpretability claims are unsupported\n117\t2. **Unnamed embedding model is a reproducibility failure**\n118\t3. **Related work is severely incomplete** — missing PRMs, RL value functions, agent self-reflection\n119\t4. **No statistical rigor** — single seed, no confidence intervals\n120\t5. **Novelty is thin** — R1 acknowledges but sees value in the weak-supervision pipeline if executed cleanly; R2 considers it insufficient for a top venue\n121\t\n122\tDisagreements:\n123\t- R1 rates presentation higher (4/5) while R2 rates it lower (3/5 → 2-3/5 after cross-sharing)\n124\t- R1 sees more novelty in LF temporal dynamics; R2 considers it descriptive only\n125\t- R2 is harsher on novelty (2/5 contribution) vs R1 (3/5)\n126\t\n127\t---\n128\t\n129\t## 4. Consolidated Assessment\n130\t\n131\t### What the Paper Does Well\n132\t- **Problem is real and practically important.** Semantic stagnation is a genuine failure mode in deployed LLM agents.\n133\t- **Experimental coverage is exceptional.** 16 experiments, 7 baselines, 6 ablations — well above average thoroughness.\n134\t- **Evaluation design is mostly sound.** Human gold set as primary evaluation, instance-level splitting, cost-efficiency Pareto analysis.\n135\t- **\"Student exceeds teacher\" result is interesting.** The +4.43pp over LF oracle demonstrates the GRU captures temporal patterns beyond its training signal.\n136\t- **LF temporal dynamics observation** (leading/lagging indicators, onset agreement 34% → 71%) is a genuinely interesting finding about data programming on sequential data.\n137\t\n138\t### Critical Issues Requiring Resolution\n139\t\n140\t| # | Issue | Severity | Both Agree? |\n141\t|---|-------|----------|-------------|\n142\t| 1 | Code-paper discrepancy (no attention in code, BiGRU+attention in paper) | **Fatal** | Yes |\n143\t| 2 | Sentence encoder unnamed, no ablation | Critical | Yes |\n144\t| 3 | No multi-seed variance or significance tests | Critical | Yes |\n145\t| 4 | Related work incomplete (10 refs, missing PRMs, RL, Reflexion) | Critical | Yes |\n146\t| 5 | Novelty = Snorkel + GRU, both off-the-shelf | Major | Yes (degree differs) |\n147\t| 6 | Domain specificity (SWE-bench only, no cross-task eval) | Major | Yes |\n148\t| 7 | Human gold set too small for narrow margins | Major | Yes |\n149\t| 8 | Cross-agent eval on unvalidated LF-generated labels | Major | Yes |\n150\t| 9 | outcome_backpropagation future information leakage | Moderate | Yes |\n151\t| 10 | Repeat-3 straw-man baseline | Moderate | R1 only |\n152\t\n153\t### Experiment Coverage vs. Design Reference\n154\t\n155\tThe paper covers all 16 imagined experiments from review_context.yaml, which is commendable. However, coverage is necessary but not sufficient — the quality of execution (issues #1–3 above) undermines the otherwise thorough experimental program.\n156\t\n157\t**Missing from imagined experiments that would strengthen the paper:**\n158\t- Multi-seed variance (mentioned in experiment plans but not executed)\n159\t- Embedding model ablation\n160\t- Human labels on OpenHands subset\n161\t- PRM baseline comparison\n162\t- Soft-match cycle detection baseline\n163\t\n164\t### Scores Summary\n165\t\n166\t| Criterion | R1 | R2 | Consolidated |\n167\t|-----------|-----|-----|-------------|\n168\t| Soundness | 3/5 | 3/5 | **3/5** |\n169\t| Presentation | 4/5 | 3/5 | **3/5** |\n170\t| Contribution | 3/5 | 2/5 | **2.5/5** |\n171\t| Overall | 3/6 | 2/6 (post-discussion) | **3/6 (Reject)** |\n172\t| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | **4/5** |\n173\t\n174\t### Final Recommendation: **Reject (3/6)**\n175\t\n176\tThe paper addresses a real, practically important problem and presents an impressively thorough experimental program. However, multiple critical issues prevent acceptance:\n177\t\n178\t1. The **code-paper architecture discrepancy** is the most serious concern. The described BiGRU+attention model does not exist in the codebase; all attention-based interpretability results are therefore unsupported. This must be resolved before any re-evaluation.\n179\t2. The **unnamed embedding model** and **absence of multi-seed variance** are basic reproducibility and rigor gaps below the threshold for a top venue.\n180\t3. The **novelty is insufficient** — applying Snorkel majority-vote + training a GRU classifier are both standard techniques. The paper's contribution is primarily empirical/engineering, which demands even higher standards for experimental rigor (currently undermined by issues #1–3).\n181\t4. The **related work is severely incomplete** — failure to engage with process reward models, RL value functions, and agent self-reflection methods leaves the paper's positioning unclear.\n182\t\n183\t### Path to Acceptance\n184\t\n185\tThe paper has a viable path to acceptance if the authors:\n186\t1. **Align code and paper** — implement the described BiGRU+attention, or correct the paper to describe the actual model; re-run all experiments with the final architecture\n187\t2. **Name and ablate the embedding model** — at minimum test 2–3 encoders\n188\t3. **Report multi-seed variance** (≥3 seeds) with confidence intervals on gold set results\n189\t4. **Expand related work to 30+ references** — engage deeply with PRMs, RL value functions, agent scaffolding (Reflexion/LATS), and weak supervision advances\n190\t5. **Add human labels for OpenHands** (even 100–200 windows) to validate cross-agent claims\n191\t6. **Evaluate on one non-coding agent domain** to strengthen generality\n192\t7. **Address outcome_backpropagation leakage** — report fraction of exit_context data and test sensitivity\n193\t\n194\tWith these revisions, the comprehensive experimental program and practical relevance would make a solid submission.\n195\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QprabZCkkXQP6cEHHGFc4H","session_id":"16ab1c0f-3ad5-4e32-8f39-ad0da20f8dab","uuid":"dd8f56c8-fe04-46e9-94ac-3e36ad78b24c","timestamp":"2026-05-14T09:05:43.097Z"}
