{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014PjH6ymv839jSpZsaUGT3C","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage{url}\n13\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n14\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n15\t\n16\t\\title{ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation\\\\for LLM Agent Trajectories}\n17\t\n18\t\\author{Anonymous}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t% ============================================================\n24\t% ABSTRACT\n25\t% ============================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tLLM-based agents frequently exhibit \\emph{semantic stagnation} during long-horizon tasks---executing superficially diverse but collectively ineffective action sequences that waste computational budget without advancing toward the goal.\n28\tDetecting such stagnation is difficult: actions are non-repetitive at the surface level, and step-wise scoring methods lose the temporal dynamics of progress within a window of steps.\n29\tWe propose \\textsc{ProgressProbe}, a framework that combines (1)~a \\emph{data-programming} weak-supervision pipeline that transfers the Snorkel paradigm to sequential agent trajectories, producing window-level progress labels from three domain-specific labeling functions, and (2)~a lightweight GRU probe with attention that learns temporal progress signals from these noisy labels.\n30\tOn SWE-bench agent trajectories, ProgressProbe achieves 0.91 macro-F1 on majority-vote labels and 0.86 macro-F1 on a 400-window human-annotated gold set, outperforming the best heuristic baseline (Repeat-3) by +6.24\\,pp and +6.89\\,pp respectively.\n31\tThe probe surpasses even the labeling-function ensemble oracle (+4.43\\,pp) and a few-shot Claude Sonnet~4 judge (+3.74\\,pp) while being 606$\\times$ faster and 31$\\times$ cheaper.\n32\tA zero-shot cross-agent transfer from SWE-agent to OpenHands retains 0.83 macro-F1, confirming that the learned signal captures general goal-directed progress rather than agent-specific behavioral patterns.\n33\tSimulated early termination driven by the probe saves 32.7\\% of tokens while preserving 96.1\\% of task success rate.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t% ============================================================\n37\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n38\t% ============================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\n41\tLLM-powered autonomous agents~\\cite{yang2024swe,wang2024opendevin} increasingly tackle complex, multi-step tasks such as software engineering, data analysis, and web navigation.\n42\tA persistent failure mode in these systems is \\emph{semantic stagnation}: the agent continues to act---issuing diverse commands, editing files, reading documentation---yet makes no meaningful progress toward its goal.\n43\tUnlike simple infinite loops or exact command repetition, semantic stagnation is characterized by surface-level diversity that masks underlying ineffectiveness, making it invisible to cycle detectors and structurally repetitive action filters.\n44\t\n45\tExisting approaches to monitoring agent progress fall into three categories, none of which fully address the problem.\n46\t\\emph{Cycle detectors}~\\cite{chen2024codeagent,guo2024specra} identify structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation.\n47\t\\emph{Step-level value estimators}~\\cite{luo2024agentprm,chen2024swetrace} score individual actions and aggregate uniformly, losing the temporal signature of stagnation onset within a window.\n48\t\\emph{Self-reflection mechanisms}~\\cite{zhang2024agentcollab} rely on the stagnating agent itself to diagnose the problem---a fundamentally unreliable signal.\n49\t\n50\tWe reframe progress stagnation detection as a \\emph{window-level progress estimation} problem and make two contributions:\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{A data-programming framework for agent trajectories.}\n53\tWe design three labeling functions (LFs)---\\texttt{file\\_diff\\_delta}, \\texttt{command\\_repetition}, and \\texttt{outcome\\_backpropagation}---that capture complementary dimensions of progress and stagnation.\n54\tTheir majority vote produces window-level labels that achieve 84.5\\% accuracy against human annotations (Fleiss $\\kappa=0.724$).\n55\tThis constitutes a non-trivial transfer of the Snorkel paradigm~\\cite{ratner2017snorkel} to sequential trajectory data, where LFs exhibit temporal dynamics (leading vs.\\ lagging indicators) absent in i.i.d.\\ settings.\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Empirical evidence that temporal modeling matters.}\n58\tA lightweight GRU probe with attention, trained on these weakly-supervised labels, achieves 0.91 macro-F1---outperforming the LF ensemble oracle by 4.43\\,pp, demonstrating that the probe learns richer representations beyond the rules that generated its training signal.\n59\tThe 13.72\\,pp gap over mean-pooled embeddings with logistic regression confirms that temporal modeling of step-to-step transitions, not merely better features, drives the improvement.\n60\t\n61\tThe architecture is a deliberate engineering choice, not a contribution: our novelty lies in the weak-supervision methodology and the systematic empirical study across training regimes, baselines, and agent implementations.\n62\t\n63\t% ============================================================\n64\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n65\t% ============================================================\n66\t\\section{Related Work}\n67\t\n68\t\\paragraph{Agent monitoring and intervention.}\n69\tRecent work on detecting failure modes in LLM agents spans cycle detection~\\cite{chen2024codeagent}, trajectory anomaly detection~\\cite{guo2024specra}, and process reward models~\\cite{luo2024agentprm,chen2024swetrace}.\n70\tThese approaches either target structural repetition (missing semantic stagnation) or operate at step granularity (losing window-level temporal dynamics).\n71\tProgressProbe operates at the window level, capturing stagnation onset patterns that step-level methods average away.\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Data programming and weak supervision.}\n74\tThe Snorkel framework~\\cite{ratner2017snorkel} introduced labeling functions for programmatic data annotation in NLP.\n75\tExtensions have been applied to relation extraction, sentiment analysis, and medical NLP~\\cite{ratner2019weaksuper}.\n76\tOur work is, to our knowledge, the first to apply data programming to sequential agent trajectory data, where the temporal ordering of LF activations introduces dynamics not present in the original i.i.d.\\ formulation.\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Probing learned representations.}\n79\tProbing classifiers are widely used to analyze what information is encoded in neural representations~\\cite{belinkov2022probing}.\n80\tWe adopt the probing methodology not for analysis but for \\emph{deployment}: the probe is a lightweight, real-time progress estimator trained on frozen embeddings.\n81\t\n82\t% ============================================================\n83\t% 3. METHOD\n84\t% ============================================================\n85\t\\section{Method}\n86\t\n87\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n88\t\n89\tGiven an agent trajectory $\\tau = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_T)$ where each $a_t$ is an action (command execution, file edit, etc.), we define a \\emph{sliding window} $w_i = (a_i, a_{i+1}, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})$ of $k$ consecutive steps.\n90\tThe task is binary classification: assign each window a label $y \\in \\{\\texttt{progress}, \\texttt{stagnation}\\}$.\n91\tWe use $k=5$ by default (see Section~\\ref{sec:ablations} for ablation).\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Weak Supervision via Labeling Functions}\n94\t\\label{sec:lfs}\n95\t\n96\tWe design three labeling functions, each capturing a distinct dimension of progress:\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{\\texttt{file\\_diff\\_delta}.}\n99\tMeasures the magnitude of file changes across the window.\n100\tIf the cumulative diff size decreases or remains zero across consecutive steps, the LF votes \\texttt{stagnation}.\n101\tThis is the strongest individual signal (Section~\\ref{sec:lf-ablation}), directly measuring output change.\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{\\texttt{command\\_repetition}.}\n104\tDetects whether the agent repeats semantically equivalent commands.\n105\tBeyond exact string matching, it uses embedding similarity to identify paraphrased commands.\n106\tThis LF is the earliest leading indicator of stagnation, firing on average 1.8 steps before the other LFs reach consensus (Table~\\ref{tab:lf-dynamics}).\n107\t\n108\t\\paragraph{\\texttt{outcome\\_backpropagation}.}\n109\tPropagates the final trajectory outcome backward: if the trajectory ultimately fails and the window shows no corrective action, it votes \\texttt{stagnation}.\n110\tThis is a lagging indicator (average lag: 2.1 steps) that provides complementary signal from the future.\n111\t\n112\t\\paragraph{Label aggregation.}\n113\tWe aggregate LF votes via majority vote.\n114\tWindows where at least 2 of 3 LFs agree (conf$\\geq$2) form a high-confidence subset; the remaining conf=1 windows have only a single LF vote and higher noise (~25--30\\% error rate).\n115\t\n116\t\\subsection{Embedding Extraction}\n117\t\n118\tEach action $a_t$ is embedded using a frozen sentence encoder to obtain $\\mathbf{e}_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^{1024}$.\n119\tA window $w_i$ is represented as a sequence $(\\mathbf{e}_i, \\ldots, \\mathbf{e}_{i+k-1})$.\n120\t\n121\t\\subsection{GRU Probe with Attention}\n122\t\n123\tThe probe processes the embedding sequence through a single-layer bidirectional GRU:\n124\t\\begin{equation}\n125\t\\mathbf{h}_t = \\text{BiGRU}(\\mathbf{e}_t, \\mathbf{h}_{t-1})\n126\t\\end{equation}\n127\tAn attention mechanism computes a weighted summary:\n128\t\\begin{equation}\n129\t\\alpha_t = \\frac{\\exp(\\mathbf{v}^\\top \\tanh(\\mathbf{W}\\mathbf{h}_t))}{\\sum_{j=1}^{k}\\exp(\\mathbf{v}^\\top \\tanh(\\mathbf{W}\\mathbf{h}_j))}\n130\t\\end{equation}\n131\t\\begin{equation}\n132\t\\mathbf{c} = \\sum_{t=1}^{k} \\alpha_t \\mathbf{h}_t\n133\t\\end{equation}\n134\tThe context vector $\\mathbf{c}$ is passed through a linear classifier.\n135\tThe model has 1.32M parameters and trains in $\\sim$12 minutes.\n136\t\n137\t\\subsection{Training Objective}\n138\t\n139\tWe train with cross-entropy loss augmented by a supervised contrastive (SupCon) auxiliary loss with weight $\\lambda=0.3$ and temperature $\\tau=0.07$:\n140\t\\begin{equation}\n141\t\\mathcal{L} = \\mathcal{L}_\\text{CE} + \\lambda \\cdot \\mathcal{L}_\\text{SupCon}\n142\t\\end{equation}\n143\tSupCon encourages same-class hidden states to cluster and different-class states to separate, improving embedding separation from 0.761 to 0.847 (Table~\\ref{tab:supcon}).\n144\t\n145\t% ============================================================\n146\t% 4. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n147\t% ============================================================\n148\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n149\t\n150\t\\paragraph{Data.}\n151\tWe extract trajectories from SWE-bench Verified runs of SWE-agent, yielding 54,809 windows (conf$\\geq$1) of which 15,401 are high-confidence (conf$\\geq$2).\n152\tFor cross-agent evaluation, we process 2,195 OpenHands instances producing 48,352 windows.\n153\t\n154\t\\paragraph{Human-annotated gold set.}\n155\tThree annotators independently label 400 windows (156 stagnation, 244 progress).\n156\tInter-annotator agreement is Fleiss $\\kappa=0.724$ (substantial); 68.2\\% of windows achieve unanimous agreement.\n157\tMajority-vote labels match human gold labels with 84.5\\% accuracy.\n158\t\n159\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n160\tWe compare against:\n161\t(1)~\\textbf{Repeat-3}: heuristic detecting $\\geq$3 consecutive identical actions;\n162\t(2)~\\textbf{Cosine Similarity}: pairwise embedding cosine between consecutive steps;\n163\t(3)~\\textbf{LF Oracle}: direct majority vote of LF outputs at test time;\n164\t(4)~\\textbf{Mean-pool + LR/SVM}: mean-pooled window embeddings with logistic regression or RBF-SVM;\n165\t(5)~\\textbf{Max-pool + LR}: max-pooled embeddings with LR;\n166\t(6)~\\textbf{LLM-as-Judge}: Claude Sonnet~4 and GPT-4o in zero-shot and few-shot settings;\n167\t(7)~\\textbf{Non-NLP (GBT)}: gradient-boosted trees on 18 hand-crafted structural features.\n168\t\n169\t\\paragraph{Metrics.}\n170\tWe report macro-F1, per-class F1 for stagnation and progress, and coverage.\n171\t\n172\t% ============================================================\n173\t% 5. RESULTS\n174\t% ============================================================\n175\t\\section{Results}\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Main Results on Human Gold Set}\n178\t\\label{sec:gold}\n179\t\n180\tTable~\\ref{tab:gold} presents results on the 400-window human-annotated gold set, providing evaluation free of circular dependency with the weak-supervision labels.\n181\t\n182\t\\begin{table}[t]\n183\t\\centering\n184\t\\small\n185\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n186\t\\toprule\n187\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{F1-S} & \\textbf{F1-P} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$R3} \\\\\n188\t\\midrule\n189\tGRU Probe & \\textbf{.862} & \\textbf{.809} & \\textbf{.916} & +6.89 \\\\\n190\tClaude S4 (fs) & .841 & .783 & .899 & +4.78 \\\\\n191\tLF Oracle & .819 & .751 & .886 & +2.53 \\\\\n192\tRepeat-3 & .793 & .720 & .867 & --- \\\\\n193\tMean-pool+LR & .729 & .623 & .834 & $-$6.45 \\\\\n194\t\\bottomrule\n195\t\\end{tabular}\n196\t\\caption{Results on 400-window human-annotated gold set. F1 = macro-F1; F1-S/F1-P = stagnation/progress F1; $\\Delta$R3 = gap vs.\\ Repeat-3 in pp.}\n197\t\\label{tab:gold}\n198\t\\end{table}\n199\t\n200\tThe probe achieves 0.862 macro-F1 on human labels, outperforming all baselines."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QprabZCkkXQP6cEHHGFc4H","session_id":"16ab1c0f-3ad5-4e32-8f39-ad0da20f8dab","uuid":"f5602bf9-e59c-47cf-af8a-cd34e5b47437","timestamp":"2026-05-14T09:05:49.250Z"}
