{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ac3bcdd59bd9b54f1","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QprabZCkkXQP6cEHHGFc4H","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","usage":{"total_tokens":90835,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":169857},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"50ba1fe8-2359-49d4-8f7b-680116b9c697","session_id":"16ab1c0f-3ad5-4e32-8f39-ad0da20f8dab"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TRCKwVpwRhMG4sgNhUccuC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PWqD58cTuvLmDXC1smAMfb","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"agent_progress_probe","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T081656","overall_score":"weak accept","report":"---\nreviewer: R1\nfocus: Methods + Experiments\noverall_score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)\nconfidence: 4/5\n---\n\n# Review: \"What Do Labeling Functions Reveal About Agent Stagnation? An Empirical Study of Weak Supervision for Sequential Agent Behavior\"\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper applies data programming (weak supervision via labeling functions) to detect stagnation in LLM agent trajectories, training a GRU probe on majority-vote labels from three LFs over 54,809 windows from SWE-bench traces. The paper's core contribution is a set of rigorously documented negative results: the probe's high-confidence performance (macro-F1 0.91) does not generalize to full data (0.74) or human gold labels (0.68); the probe fails to outperform the simple Repeat-3 heuristic on gold labels (0.72 vs 0.68); and cross-agent transfer degrades substantially (0.74 to 0.62). The paper salvages a genuinely interesting positive finding: an analysis of LF temporal dynamics revealing a \"stagnation onset signature\" with characteristic leading/lagging patterns across the three LFs, and 4 transition types covering 87% of episodes.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Exemplary honesty about negative results.** The paper systematically dismantles its own original claims with escalating levels of evaluation rigor. The progression from conf>=2 evaluation (0.91) to full data (0.74) to human gold labels (0.68) is a textbook demonstration of how selection bias inflates reported performance. This kind of transparency is rare and valuable.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental program.** All 14+ planned experiments are executed, including the ones that produce unflattering results. The paper does not cherry-pick. Tables 1-21 cover main results, baselines (7 methods), ablations (6 dimensions), error analysis, cross-agent transfer, temporal dynamics, and early termination simulation. This thoroughness is well above average.\n\n- **Selection bias diagnosis is well-supported.** Table 11 (error distribution by confidence) is the smoking gun: conf=1 windows have 31% error rate and account for 71% of all errors. The paper correctly identifies that conf>=2 filtering creates a tautologically easy subset where the LFs that generated the labels agree, making any model trained on those labels appear artificially strong. This is a genuine methodological insight for the weak supervision community.\n\n- **Human gold label evaluation is the right primary metric.** The 400-window, 3-annotator gold set (Table 3) provides the definitive evaluation. The paper correctly makes this the centerpiece rather than burying it.\n\n- **LF temporal dynamics (Section 8) is independently valuable.** The finding that command_repetition leads by 1.8 steps, file_diff_delta is concurrent, and outcome_backpropagation lags by 2.3 steps is a genuinely interesting observation about how labeling functions behave on sequential data. The 4 stagnation transition patterns (Table 14, 87% coverage) provide actionable understanding. This analysis does not depend on the probe's accuracy and stands on its own.\n\n- **Instance-level data splitting.** The paper correctly splits by instance_id to prevent train/test leakage from windows within the same trajectory. This is a detail many papers get wrong.\n\n- **LLM-as-Judge baseline provides useful calibration.** Table 5 shows GPT-4o few-shot achieves 0.84 gold-F1, substantially outperforming the probe (0.68). Rather than hiding this, the paper uses it to honestly position the probe. Table 6 provides a useful cost-accuracy Pareto analysis showing the probe's only advantage is cost ($4/yr vs $910/yr).\n\n- **FN decomposition (Table 12) is insightful.** The 58% semantic stagnation / 24% late-onset / 18% ambiguous boundary breakdown identifies specific failure modes and explains why the problem is harder than surface-level repetition detection.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) The positive finding on LF temporal dynamics, while interesting, is descriptive rather than prescriptive.** The paper identifies the stagnation onset signature and 4 transition patterns, but does not demonstrate how these findings improve any downstream system. Could these temporal patterns be used to design better LFs, train a better probe, or build a more effective early termination system? Without such a demonstration, the finding remains an observation. The paper should at minimum include a proof-of-concept showing that temporally-aware LF weighting (e.g., giving more weight to command_repetition as a leading indicator) improves label quality.\n\n- **(Major) The GRU vs Mean-pool+LR gap is not statistically significant.** Table 7 shows the GRU achieves 0.74 on full data vs Mean-pool+LR at 0.72 (+2pp), with p approximately 0.08-0.15. The paper reports ~530K params and 28 min training for GRU vs ~1K params and 12 sec for Mean-pool+LR. If the gap is not significant, the paper's implicit claim that temporal modeling provides value (even modest value) on full data is unsupported. This needs bootstrap confidence intervals or a multi-seed analysis.\n\n- **(Major) No multi-seed variance reported.** All results appear to be from a single seed. Given the narrow margins -- probe vs Repeat-3 on gold is -4pp (0.68 vs 0.72), probe vs Mean-pool+LR on full data is +2pp -- variance estimates are essential. Without them, we cannot determine whether the probe's underperformance vs Repeat-3 on gold labels is a robust finding or within noise.\n\n- **(Moderate) The paper does not name the sentence encoder.** Section 3 mentions \"a frozen sentence encoder\" with 1024-d outputs but never identifies the model. From the project files this appears to be Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B, but this is a basic reproducibility gap. Given that the entire probe pipeline operates on these embeddings, the choice of encoder could substantially affect results. An embedding model ablation (even 2 encoders) would strengthen the paper.\n\n- **(Moderate) outcome_backpropagation LF leaks future information.** This LF propagates the final trajectory outcome backward to label earlier windows. For failed trajectories, this means windows that may contain genuine progress are labeled as stagnation simply because the trajectory ultimately failed. The paper does not report what fraction of training data comes from exit_context trajectories or analyze the sensitivity to this LF's inclusion. Table 15 (LF hold-one-out) shows removing outcome_backprop has the smallest effect, which is consistent with it being either minimally useful or introducing systematic bias that the other LFs compensate for -- but the paper does not disambiguate.\n\n- **(Moderate) Cross-agent transfer evaluation lacks human validation.** The OpenHands results (Table 9) use LF-generated labels as ground truth, but these LFs required format adaptation. Without any human labels on OpenHands windows, the 0.62 macro-F1 may reflect LF adaptation quality rather than true probe transfer performance. Even 100 human-labeled OpenHands windows would greatly strengthen this section.\n\n- **(Moderate) Repeat-3 as the primary heuristic baseline uses exact string matching only.** A soft-match variant using embedding similarity (e.g., cosine > 0.9 for 3 consecutive steps) would be a stronger and fairer baseline. The fact that even exact-match Repeat-3 beats the probe on gold labels (0.72 vs 0.68) suggests that a soft-match version might further widen the gap, which would sharpen the negative result but also raise questions about whether the weak supervision framework adds value at all.\n\n- **(Minor) The human annotation gold set is small for the margins involved.** 400 windows (156 stag, 244 prog) yields approximately +/-3-4pp bootstrap CI on macro-F1. The 4pp gap between Repeat-3 (0.72) and probe (0.68) is within this uncertainty. Inter-annotator agreement on semantic stagnation (kappa=0.38, Table 4) and ambiguous cases (kappa=0.29) is low, suggesting that the gold standard itself has noise.\n\n- **(Minor) Related work coverage is thin.** 10 references is far below the 30-50 expected for a top venue. Critical missing areas: process reward models (Lightman et al. 2023, Math-Shepherd), RL value functions as progress estimators, agent self-reflection frameworks (Reflexion, LATS), weak supervision advances beyond Snorkel (MeTaL, FlyingSquid).\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. What is the frozen sentence encoder used for embedding extraction? How sensitive are results to this choice?\n\n2. Can you provide multi-seed variance (>=3 seeds) for the key comparisons: probe vs Repeat-3 on gold labels, probe vs Mean-pool+LR on full data, and cross-agent transfer?\n\n3. What fraction of training windows derive from exit_context trajectories where outcome_backpropagation labels ALL windows as stagnation? Have you tested training without this LF and evaluating on gold labels?\n\n4. The stagnation onset signature (Section 8) is the paper's strongest positive finding. Have you attempted to use these temporal dynamics to improve label quality -- e.g., by weighting command_repetition more heavily at early window positions?\n\n5. How were the 400 gold windows sampled? Stratified by confidence level? If not, what is the confidence distribution in the gold set?\n\n6. For the early termination simulation (Table 21), the probe achieves only 0.68 gold-F1. How do the token savings change when evaluated against gold labels rather than LF labels? The 32.7% savings figure may be inflated.\n\n7. Table 4 shows inter-annotator kappa for semantic stagnation is only 0.38. This suggests the task itself may not be well-defined at the window level. Have you considered whether a coarser granularity (trajectory-level or episode-level) might yield more reliable labels?\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- Table 7: The p-value range (0.08-0.15) for GRU vs Mean-pool+LR should be reported precisely, not as a range.\n- The paper uses both \"macro-F1\" and \"Macro-F1\" inconsistently.\n- Table 3: The LF Oracle achieves 0.65 gold-F1, which is 27pp below its LF-label F1 of 0.92. This massive drop is the strongest evidence of circular evaluation but deserves more discussion -- it shows the LF labels are a poor proxy for true performance.\n- The \"stagnation onset signature\" terminology should be defined more precisely. Is onset defined as the first window in a contiguous stagnation span? The first window where any LF fires stagnation?\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThis paper makes a valuable contribution through its honest, systematic documentation of negative results and its well-supported diagnosis of selection bias in weak supervision for sequential data. The negative results themselves are informative: they demonstrate concretely that (1) high-confidence weak-supervision subsets create tautologically easy evaluation sets, (2) simple heuristics remain competitive with learned probes when evaluated on human labels, and (3) cross-agent transfer of weakly-supervised models is fragile. These are lessons the community needs.\n\nThe LF temporal dynamics analysis provides a genuinely novel observation about how labeling functions behave on sequential data, distinct from the i.i.d. setting assumed by standard data programming. The 4 transition patterns and the leading/lagging indicator structure are independently interesting.\n\nThe paper falls short in three areas: (1) the positive finding remains descriptive without a prescriptive demonstration; (2) the absence of multi-seed variance makes it impossible to assess whether narrow margins are robust; and (3) the unnamed embedding model and thin related work section are below the standard for a top venue. Despite these gaps, the experimental thoroughness (16 experiments, 7 baselines, 6 ablations) and the intellectual honesty elevate this above the typical negative-results paper.\n\nI lean toward acceptance because the negative results are well-executed and genuinely informative, and the LF temporal dynamics observation opens a new research direction. However, multi-seed variance and the embedding model identification are necessary revisions.\n\n**Confidence: 4/5** (I have examined the code, data pipeline, experimental design, and project history in detail. I am confident in my assessment of the methodology and the gap between code and paper claims. My uncertainty is primarily about whether the community will value the negative results and descriptive findings sufficiently for a top venue.)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":759,"cache_read_input_tokens":89501,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":759,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QprabZCkkXQP6cEHHGFc4H","session_id":"16ab1c0f-3ad5-4e32-8f39-ad0da20f8dab","uuid":"905732f0-d593-4740-ac52-565940137914"}
