{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VgSbjk31e3e87RE4tJELyi","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary of my assessment:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key findings as R2 (Novelty & Related Work):**\n\n**Strengths identified:**\n- Practically relevant problem (semantic stagnation is a real failure mode)\n- \"Student exceeds teacher\" effect (+4.43pp over LF oracle) is the cleanest contribution\n- Strong cost-efficiency argument vs LLM-as-Judge (31x cheaper, 606x faster, higher F1)\n- Cross-agent transfer provides genuine generalization evidence\n- Exceptional experimental coverage (16 experiments, 7 baselines, 6 ablations)\n\n**Critical weaknesses:**\n1. **Novelty is thin** -- vanilla Snorkel majority voting (no label model, no temporal adaptation) + standard GRU classifier. The \"first to apply data programming to sequential data\" claim is undercut by the fact that no sequential adaptation was made to the data programming step itself. LF temporal dynamics is descriptive only, not methodological.\n\n2. **Related work severely incomplete** -- only 10 references. Missing engagement with: Process Reward Models (Lightman et al., Math-Shepherd), RL value functions, Reflexion/LATS, weak supervision advances (MeTaL, FlyingSquid), noisy label learning, active learning alternatives. This is the largest single gap.\n\n3. **Domain specificity undermines generality** -- all LFs and evaluation are SWE-bench specific. Cross-agent transfer within the same task distribution is a weak form of generalization. No non-coding evaluation.\n\n**Additional concerns:** code-paper architecture discrepancy (no attention in code, BiGRU+attention in paper), single-seed evaluation, outcome_backpropagation future leakage, underspecified LLM-as-Judge comparison, venue fit (SE-specific work at NLP venue).\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T081656/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5ee6aa948fbb6daa (use SendMessage with to: 'a5ee6aa948fbb6daa' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 93137\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 208592</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"16ab1c0f-3ad5-4e32-8f39-ad0da20f8dab","uuid":"61e12df7-ab39-4ce8-b93c-62e0f56d6dbe","timestamp":"2026-05-14T09:09:05.764Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a blind peer review for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Paper: \"What Do Labeling Functions Reveal About Agent Stagnation? An Empirical Study of Weak Supervision for Sequential Agent Behavior\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nThe paper applies data programming (weak supervision) to detect stagnation in LLM agent trajectories. Three labeling functions (LFs) are designed: command_repetition, file_diff_delta, outcome_backpropagation. A GRU-based probe is trained on majority-vote labels. The paper's core narrative is **negative results**: all three original claims fail (generalization, outperforming heuristics, cross-agent transfer). Positive finding: LF temporal dynamics analysis reveals a \"stagnation onset signature\" and 4 stagnation transition patterns.\n\n### Paper Structure\n1. Introduction — frames as empirical study of where/why data programming fails on sequential agent data\n2. Related Work — covers Agent Monitoring/Intervention, Data Programming/Weak Supervision, LLM-as-Judge (3 short paragraphs)\n3. Framework and Method — task formulation, 3 LFs, GRU probe, data (54,809 windows from SWE-bench)\n4. Generalization Failure — conf≥2 selection bias, LF oracle comparison, human gold evaluation\n5. Baseline Comparisons — LLM-as-Judge, Mean-pool+LR, Non-NLP features\n6. Cross-Agent Transfer — SWE-agent → OpenHands (12pp drop)\n7. Error Analysis — conf-stratified errors, FN taxonomy\n8. LF Temporal Dynamics — temporal ordering (leading/concurrent/lagging), 4 transition patterns, marginal contributions\n9. Ablation Studies — window size, architecture, SupCon, data scaling, representation quality\n10. Application: Simulated Early Termination\n11. Discussion — \"what went wrong\" (LF temporal correlation violates i.i.d.), \"what went right\" (LF dynamics), \"when is probe useful\" (cost-constrained)\n12. Limitations — domain scope, binary labels, gold set size, LF coverage, embedding quality\n13. Conclusion\n\nReferences: only 5 cited papers (Yang et al. 2024 SWE-bench, Wang et al. 2024 OpenDevin, Ratner et al. 2017 Snorkel, Zheng et al. 2024 LLM-as-judge, Khosla et al. 2020 SupCon)\n\n### Key Claims and Contributions\nThe paper claims 3 contributions:\n1. First large-scale weak-supervision labeling framework for agent stagnation, including LF temporal dynamics taxonomy\n2. Systematic characterization of the failure mode where conf≥2 performance doesn't generalize to full data or gold labels\n3. Battery of negative results (cross-agent transfer, architecture ablations, data scaling, early termination) delineating limits of lightweight probes\n\n### Key Experimental Results\n- conf≥2 F1 0.91 drops to 0.74 (full data) and 0.68 (gold labels)\n- Probe underperforms Repeat-3 on gold labels (0.68 vs 0.72)\n- LF Oracle outperforms probe on conf≥2 (0.92 vs 0.91)\n- LLM-as-Judge achieves 0.86 macro-F1 (12pp above probe) at 181x cost\n- Mean-pool+LR achieves 0.72 (only 2pp below probe, not significant)\n- Cross-agent transfer: 0.74→0.62 (12pp drop)\n- Stagnation onset signature: cmd_rep leads 1.8 steps, file_diff concurrent, outcome_backprop lags 2.3 steps\n- 4 transition patterns covering 87% of episodes\n- Fleiss κ=0.52 for human annotation (moderate agreement)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper focusing on:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- Is the problem formulation novel? (applying data programming to agent trajectory labeling)\n- Are the negative findings genuinely new and surprising? Or are they predictable?\n- Is the LF temporal dynamics analysis a genuine contribution?\n- Is the stagnation taxonomy (4 types) novel and useful?\n- Does the paper advance our understanding of agent monitoring or data programming?\n- How does this compare to simply running existing data programming tools (e.g., Snorkel) on this data?\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n- The paper only cites 5 references. Is this adequate for a top-venue submission?\n- Missing related work areas to check:\n  - Agent monitoring beyond cycle detection (process reward models, outcome-based reward, etc.)\n  - Weak supervision beyond Snorkel (MeTaL, FlyingSquid, other label model improvements)\n  - Programmatic labeling for sequential/temporal data\n  - Agent self-improvement and reflection (Reflexion, etc.)\n  - Trajectory evaluation and reward modeling\n  - Distribution shift / domain adaptation in NLP\n  - Noisy label learning literature\n  - Active learning as alternative to weak supervision\n- Is the positioning against existing work fair and accurate?\n\n### 3. Significance and Impact\n- Who benefits from these findings?\n- Are the negative results actionable? Do they change what practitioners would do?\n- Is the stagnation taxonomy useful for the agent monitoring community?\n- Is this a \"niche\" contribution or broadly relevant?\n- Is the paper positioned correctly as an \"empirical study\" rather than a methods paper?\n\n### 4. Framing and Narrative\n- Is the \"negative results\" framing convincing and well-executed?\n- Does the paper successfully pivot from failed claims to valuable empirical insights?\n- Is the balance between negative results and positive findings appropriate?\n- Does the Discussion section (what went wrong / what went right) add genuine insight?\n\n### 5. Venue Fit\n- Is this suitable for a top ML venue (e.g., EMNLP, NeurIPS, ICML)?\n- Would Findings track (EMNLP) be more appropriate?\n- Is the scope sufficient for a main-track submission?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Minor Issues\n- Overall Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)","agentId":"a5ee6aa948fbb6daa","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary of my assessment:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key findings as R2 (Novelty & Related Work):**\n\n**Strengths identified:**\n- Practically relevant problem (semantic stagnation is a real failure mode)\n- \"Student exceeds teacher\" effect (+4.43pp over LF oracle) is the cleanest contribution\n- Strong cost-efficiency argument vs LLM-as-Judge (31x cheaper, 606x faster, higher F1)\n- Cross-agent transfer provides genuine generalization evidence\n- Exceptional experimental coverage (16 experiments, 7 baselines, 6 ablations)\n\n**Critical weaknesses:**\n1. **Novelty is thin** -- vanilla Snorkel majority voting (no label model, no temporal adaptation) + standard GRU classifier. The \"first to apply data programming to sequential data\" claim is undercut by the fact that no sequential adaptation was made to the data programming step itself. LF temporal dynamics is descriptive only, not methodological.\n\n2. **Related work severely incomplete** -- only 10 references. Missing engagement with: Process Reward Models (Lightman et al., Math-Shepherd), RL value functions, Reflexion/LATS, weak supervision advances (MeTaL, FlyingSquid), noisy label learning, active learning alternatives. This is the largest single gap.\n\n3. **Domain specificity undermines generality** -- all LFs and evaluation are SWE-bench specific. Cross-agent transfer within the same task distribution is a weak form of generalization. No non-coding evaluation.\n\n**Additional concerns:** code-paper architecture discrepancy (no attention in code, BiGRU+attention in paper), single-seed evaluation, outcome_backpropagation future leakage, underspecified LLM-as-Judge comparison, venue fit (SE-specific work at NLP venue).\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T081656/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":208592,"totalTokens":93137,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4205,"cache_read_input_tokens":88431,"output_tokens":500,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4205},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":88431,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4205,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4205,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C9P2G1acwtMxKCPge5tJSv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I have both reviews. Let me now synthesize the roundtable discussion:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments): Score 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence 4**\n- Strengths: Honest negative results, comprehensive experiments (16 total), well-supported selection bias, LF temporal dynamics novel, proper evaluation\n- Weaknesses: LF dynamics is descriptive not prescriptive, GRU vs mean-pool not significant, no multi-seed variance, unnamed sentence encoder, outcome_backprop leaks future info, no human validation on OpenHands, thin related work\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work): Score 3 (Weak Reject), Confidence 4**\n\n\n- Strengths: Practical problem, cost-efficiency argument, cross-agent evidence, exceptional experimental coverage\n- Weaknesses: Thin novelty (vanilla Snorkel + standard GRU), severely incomplete related work (5 refs), domain-specific, code-paper architecture discrepancy, single-seed, outcome_backprop leakage, venue fit concerns\n\nWait, I need to double-check R2's claim about the student exceeding the teacher — they cited +4.43pp over the LF oracle, but looking at the actual results, the probe is actually lower than oracle on conf≥2 (0.9055 vs 0.92), and on full data it's only 0.74 vs 0.73, which is just 1pp improvement.\n\nR2 seems to have hallucinated that +4.43pp number — the paper's actual narrative is that the probe barely matches or slightly underperforms the oracle, not exceeds it. I should flag this factual error in the roundtable discussion. Also, R2 claims 10 references but the paper only has 5, so there's another discrepancy to note.\n\nNow I'm moving into the roundtable synthesis phase. Both reviewers agree on the main issues: the related work is sparse with just 5 references, there's no multi-seed variance reporting, and the outcome_backpropagation method has potential information leakage problems. Let me consolidate these shared concerns along with the factual corrections I've identified. I'm catching a critical error in R2's review — they cite a +4.43pp improvement of the student over the teacher, but when I check the actual tables, the GRU probe is only +1pp above the LF oracle on full data, and actually below it on the confidence-filtered subset. R2 seems to have either miscalculated or misread the numbers. Similarly, their efficiency claims of \"31x cheaper, 606x faster\" don't match the paper's reported 181x cost and 711x latency differences. Now I need to structure a consolidated meta-review that flags these factual discrepancies while synthesizing both reviewers' perspectives.\n\nLet me map out how the paper's experiments align with what was originally imagined — the full-data training, oracle baselines, LLM-as-Judge comparisons, mean-pooling regression, error analysis, and cross-agent transfer all appear to be covered across the relevant sections and tables.\n\nNow I'm checking the remaining experiments: the ablation studies on window size, architecture, labeling function hold-one-out, and SupCon loss, plus the non-NLP baseline, data scaling curves, simulated early stopping, interpretability visualizations, human-annotated gold sets, and temporal dynamics analysis — all of these are accounted for in their respective sections and tables as well.\n\nAll 16 experiments are covered, giving us complete coverage. Now I'm verifying the results against the expected values from the configuration file by comparing the full data metrics, oracle performance, and LLM baselines across different settings.\n\nGenerating results comparison table... Now I'm looking at the rest of the metrics table and they all check out perfectly — the numbers are identical across both versions, which makes sense since these are the designed experimental results. Moving on to write up the final review, though I'm noticing some claims from R2 that I should address carefully. They flagged a potential architecture mismatch between the code and paper regarding attention mechanisms, but without access to the actual codebase I can only go with what the paper states. There's also a discrepancy they mentioned about reference counts that doesn't quite add up.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5987,"cache_read_input_tokens":53091,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5987},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"16ab1c0f-3ad5-4e32-8f39-ad0da20f8dab","uuid":"04378235-1cd0-48c3-823c-2f971b0175ca"}
